      "problem": "Write $x^{-1/2}$ using radical notation.",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that, in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$",
